iio: buffer-dma: let userspace send DMA blocks to sockets without copying

Map the blocks with vm_insert_page() instead of remapping their PFNs. The
mapping keeps the dma_mmap_coherent() attributes, but the pages can now be
passed to the network stack with vmsplice() + splice(), which is what the
OmniCore iiod does (patches/iiod), so a block is never copied by the CPU on
its way to the Ethernet.

The stack holds page references until the data has been acknowledged (and
for retransmissions). Mapping and unmapping change refcount and mapcount
together, so any other reference means the pages are still in use:
 - an enqueued block whose pages are busy waits on a per-queue deferred list
   (polled every jiffy) before it goes back to the DMA;
 - a block whose last reference is dropped while its pages are busy is freed
   later (polled every 100 ms), never back to CMA under the stack's feet.

Without vmsplice() users nothing changes: pages are never busy.

